BT
/F1 13 Tf
50 665 Td
(Abstract & Motivation) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 645 Td
(The concept of one event happening before another in a distributed system is examined and is) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 630 Td
(shown to define a partial ordering of the events. A distributed algorithm is given for synchronizing) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 615 Td
(a system of logical clocks which can be used to totally order events.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 13 Tf
50 588 Td
(The Partial Ordering Relation (->)) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 568 Td
(The relation "->" on the set of events of a system satisfies the following conditions:) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 553 Td
(1. If a and b are events in the same process, and a comes before b, then a -> b.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 538 Td
(2. If a is the sending of a message by one process and b is its receipt by another, then a -> b.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 523 Td
(3. If a -> b and b -> c then a -> c. Two distinct events a and b are concurrent if not a -> b and not b -> a.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 13 Tf
50 496 Td
(Clock Condition Rules) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 476 Td
(Rule IR1: Each process P_i increments C_i between any two successive events.) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 461 Td
(Rule IR2: \(a\) If event a is the sending of a message m by process P_i, then message m contains timestamp T_m = C_i\(a\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 446 Td
(\(b\) Upon receiving message m, process P_j sets C_j greater than or equal to its present value and greater than T_m.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 13 Tf
50 419 Td
(Applications in State Replication) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 399 Td
(Logical clocks enable totally ordered request queues across distributed nodes,) Tj
ET
BT
/F2 10 Tf
50 384 Td
(providing fair mutex and state machine replication without centralized coordinators.) Tj
